	Changelog

	0.1.0

First release, so this is a description of what is in the tree rather than a list
of differences.

If you are here for the files: the release has four assets and three of them go
together. rvm.apk is the emulator for Android, arm64-v8a and x86_64. Image is a
riscv64 Linux 6.12 kernel with virtio-mmio, virtio-blk, virtio-net and ext4 built
in. initrd.img is a busybox initramfs that brings eth0 up and then hands the
console to /dev/vda. disk.img.gz is a Debian 13 minbase image with getty, apt,
curl and eth0 coming up at boot; unzip it first. The kernel, the initrd and the
disk have to come from the same release, so keep all three together.

Core

RV64IMAFDC, so the base integer set with M, A, F, D and the compressed
extension. Compressed instructions are expanded to their 32 bit form before
decode, which leaves one execution path for everything.

Sv39, Sv48 and Sv57 page tables in a single walk loop, superpages at every level,
hardware A and D bits and a direct mapped TLB with a generation tag.

Machine and supervisor mode with the full trap path, medeleg and mideleg,
mret, sret, wfi, sfence.vma, ecall and ebreak, plus the SBI a Linux kernel
expects (base, timer, console putchar, system reset).

Devices

ns16550a UART at 0x10000000, IRQ 10, 16 byte FIFO. CLINT at 0x02000000 with
msip, mtimecmp and a 10 MHz timebase. PLIC at 0x0c000000 with 64 sources and one
machine plus one supervisor context. Eight virtio-mmio slots at 0x10001000:
virtio-blk on slot 0 backed by a host file with pread and pwrite, virtio-net on
slot 1 with a TAP backend on Linux and a user mode NAT stack for Android.

Boot

The device tree is built in C at boot, so no DTB file is needed. dtb/rvm.dts is
the same tree written out by hand and make dtb compiles it for comparison.
The loader takes ELF64 with PT_LOAD segments or a raw blob.

The default command line is console=ttyS0 earlycon=ns16550a,mmio32,0x10000000
root=/dev/vda rootwait rw. rvm.shell=1 stays in the initramfs instead of handing
the console over, and rvm.shell=root hands it to the disk's /bin/sh without
running its init scripts. rvm.time=virtual makes the guest clock follow retired
instructions rather than the host wall clock, which is what keeps a slow phone
from looking like a stuck one.

Android

One Activity and three custom Views, no AndroidX, no Material, no Compose, no
WebView. Terminal on Canvas, graphics on SurfaceView, the release APK around
150 KB. Kernels and disk images are picked through the system file picker and
copied into the app's private storage. The app takes a partial wake lock while a
VM runs, has a machine line for RAM size and CPU count, and ships the initramfs
as an asset so only a kernel and a disk have to be picked by hand.

Tools and CI

tools/fetch_kernel.sh pulls a riscv64 kernel out of the Debian pool,
tools/mkrootfs.sh debootstraps the disk image under qemu-user-static,
tools/mkinitrd.sh builds the initramfs, tools/run.sh boots the lot and can set up
a TAP device, --gdb or a trace. build, test and android run on every push; kernel,
rootfs and release run on a v* tag and attach everything to the GitHub release.

Tests

34 suites and 677 assertions, run under gcc and clang, optimised with -Werror,
and under AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer. The harness builds a
real machine with a hand built bus, so the CPU can be tested without a VM.

Known issues

apt-get update inside the guest aborts with std::bad_alloc a few seconds in,
before it asks the network for anything. ping, DNS, curl, wget, ssh out and
dpkg all work. See PLAN, step 9 for what has been ruled out already.

virtio-gpu, virtio-input and virtio-snd are not written yet, so the guest has no
graphics or sound.
